Viewing this:

```
The only way to ensure that a user has logged out or their session has ended is to get the user's details with 
getUser(). The getClaims() method only checks local JWT validation (signature and expiration), but it doesn't
verify with the auth server whether the session is still valid or if the user has logged out server-side.
```

If it were me, here is how I would design this function internally in the class:
```ts
getUser<T>(forceRefresh = false) {

  const now = Math.floor(Date.now() / 1000);
  const exp = claims.exp;
  if (exp < now || forceRefresh) {
    return await this.getUser();
  }
  return await this.getClaims() as ReturnType<typeof getClaims> & T;
}
```

For this to work, a few things need to be true:
1. `getClaims()` and `getUser()` should really return the same type
2. the `<T>` generic will allow us to merge types with our custom type for the JWT if we have custom fields (really another problem)
3. If it is expired, or if we force refresh, it will automatically fetch the latest session with `getUser()`
4. If we are resetting a password or doing some very secure supabase call, we can force latest jwt with `getUser(true)`
5. I would also get rid of access to `getSession()`, as there is no need for it... print the depreciated message

Just some ideas...

J

For now, I've updated my example repo to handle when the session is expired, but this should probably be handled internally:
```ts
  event.locals.getUser = async () => {

    const {
      data: claimsData,
      error: claimsError
    } = await event.locals.supabase.auth.getClaims();

    if (claimsError) {
      return {
        error: claimsError,
        user: null
      };
    }

    if (!claimsData) {
      return {
        error: null,
        user: null
      };
    }

    const now = Math.floor(Date.now() / 1000);
    const exp = claimsData?.claims.exp;
    const isExpired = exp && exp < now;

    if (!isExpired) {
      return {
        error: null,
        user: claimsData.claims
      };
    }

    await event.locals.supabase.auth.signOut();

    return {
      error: null,
      user: null
    };
  }
```
This should be good enough IMO. Just remember to use `getUser(jwt)` or something if you're changing passwords or doing something that requires more security.

