In case it's useful, I wrote a slightly terser version:
```ts
event.locals.getUser = async (latestFromServer = false) => {
  const {
    data,
    error
  } = await event.locals.supabase.auth.getClaims();

  if (error || !data?.claims?.sub) return { user: null, error };

  const { claims } = data;
  let latestUser: User | undefined;

  if (latestFromServer) {
    const {
      data,
      error
    } = await event.locals.supabase.auth.getUser();

    if (error || !data.user) return { user: null, error };

    latestUser = data.user;
  }

  return {
    user: {
      ...claims,
      ...latestUser,
      id: latestUser?.id || claims.sub
    },
    error: null
  }
);
```


### @ Supabase team:

I understand the drive to mark this as resolved, but I think the discussion here is a pretty good sign that the documentation does not satisfactorily clear everything up. (Even if this issue is specifically about the documentation, I agree with @jdgamble555 that the implementation itself looks strangely convoluted and redundant.)

Considering this is about ***authentication***, we should be reaching a super high level of confidence that the docs are outlining efficient and <ins>**safe**</ins> best-practices. As it stands, this is absolutely not the case; in fact, I have to say I'm more getting the feeling that it's all vibe-coded and hastily checked, especially when the responses here only half-address the issues raised.

I'm not the best coder or anything, maybe my snippet above actually has a bunch of issues, but that's why I'd hope to be able to rely on the official documentation for safe solutions and not the help from some nice user on a random issue on github, who took the time to actually read and understand the source code. I really love using Supabase and I've appreciated how accessible it is to use and how sensible its APIs are, so I know the team can make this new auth workflow simpler to use and understand.

